sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: the end of stage 1 of the command development pipeline (docs/reference/pipeline/1-prep.mmd, "Start the build") and the start of stage 2. The pattern of task:prep-fix: one admission, one parent Swamp run, existing workflows as children. Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review.
  %% status: draft
  %% kind: tasks.build
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane VM icon=terminal pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>implementer
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>draft PR
  GW->>GH: Read the PR and reviews<br/>approval of the head
  %%> Who calls it:
  %%> - the gate check `task:pr-gate`, every minute, for a prep group whose spec is done; there is no command, route or MCP tool
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the draft PR and its reviews with a read-only App token; in manual mode the latest review of the reviewer must approve the exact head, and in automatic mode no review is read
  %%> - changes nothing on GitHub
  Note over GW: Admit the build<br/>prep group · head
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the group is open and external, with a succeeded `tasks.prep` run and no other subtask than its completed spec subtask: `waiting_for_review` after a manual prep, `running` after an automatic one or after a park that was refused
  %%> - its task VM is parked or ready with its Pi credential, and no build was admitted for it before
  %%> - the PR is open and a draft, and its head is the approved head
  %%> - no other task runs on the group; the Gateway holds one lock per group
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - records the approval (the head, the mode and the reviewer) in the intent
  %%> - appends the implementation subtask: a fixed brief that names the approved chart, page and cases and the head; the runner plan of the cases file is a command deliverable; handoff runs the configured Project check once, before the deliverables; when the Project has no check command, the subtask also has a review deliverable for the Project check
  %%> - moves the group to `running` and clears its settled time, creates the implementer thread, and captures the task VM identity, its resume only when parked, the sync and the first turn
  %%> - names the vault keys of the Pi token and of the read token
  %%> Refuses:
  %%> - a group that has started its build, a head that is not the approved one, a missing approval, or a busy group; the gate check skips it quietly and reads again at the next tick
  GW->>SW: Start one run, full intent<br/>operation-task-build
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the whole intent above and the vault key names of the Pi, VM and read-token credentials, never their values
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
  %%> - no step of the run calls the Gateway: as it starts the run, the Gateway mints the read token of the sync child, writes it and the Pi token to their vault keys, and passes only the key names
  opt the VM is parked
    SW->>VM: Resume the VM<br/>operation-task-vm-resume
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts the parked VM and waits for its agent and Pi; the workspace is as the park left it
    %%> - a ready VM skips this step and goes straight to the sync
  end
  SW->>VM: Sync the refs<br/>operation-task-sync
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - brings the default branch and the branch of the PR into `refs/remotes/origin` of the workspace, so the approved head is there; the read token comes from its vault key
  %%> - HEAD, the working tree and the local branches are not touched
  SW->>VM: Run the first turn<br/>operation-task-turn
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the first implementer turn, on a new thread, with the prompt that the Gateway composed: the title, brief and deliverables of the subtask, then the instruction to build the command
  %%> - the brief tells the implementer to merge the approved commit, fast-forward only, when HEAD differs
  alt every step succeeded
    SW-->>GW: Native terminal result<br/>capture the steps
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway for it
    Note over GW: Record the result<br/>VM · thread · head
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the task VM is recorded as ready, the metrics of the thread are recorded, and the result is the branch and the approved head
    %%> - the group stays `running` for its driver, which goes on with the check and the publish
  else a step failed
    SW--xGW: Native failed result<br/>failed step · error code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the failed step is recorded with `task_vm.resume_failed`, `tasks.sync_failed` or `tasks.turn_failed`
    %%> - the group stays `running` and the VM stays for the orchestrator; the gate check does not admit the build again
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - inspect the failed step; native resume reuses the completed ones
  end
